From 3b2e92c4fa5663714a27c9d46ba603c5fce07be1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cristopher Bautista <cristopher.abg@icloud.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 07:41:56 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] feat(llama): expose Flash Attention and KV cache
 quantization
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

llama.cpp takes both settings on llama_context_params, but nothing on Parameter reached them,
so callers ran with whatever the defaults happened to be. Both matter most on memory-limited
devices, where the KV cache — not the weights — is usually what runs out first.

Adds Parameter.flashAttention (.auto/.enabled/.disabled -> llama_flash_attn_type) and
Parameter.kvCacheType (.f16/.q8_0/.q4_0 -> ggml_type for type_k and type_v). Defaults are .auto
and .f16, which reproduce llama.cpp's own defaults exactly, so existing callers are unaffected.

The two are coupled and the docs say so: quantizing the KV cache without Flash Attention forces a
dequantize on every attention computation and can be slower than not quantizing at all.

Also adds Context.stateSizeBytes — how large a saveState(to:) file will be, and the honest way to
see what a kvCacheType actually bought.

Measured with the test model at 2048 context, cache filled before measuring (an empty context
serialises to a ~26 byte header regardless of element type, which is what made the first version
of this test pass vacuously):
  f16   11,531,586 B
  q8_0   6,128,706 B   (46.8% smaller)

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
